{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgE6cyPQ2rxAbALQzuxe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passed the self-check (`OK`). I wrote three files to `experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_0/`:\n\n- **`model_name.txt`**: `shared_judgement_personal_engagement`\n- **`hypothesis.md`**: Everyone sees randomness the same way. That shared impression is built from:\n  - how close a sequence's running heads-minus-tails tally span is to an ideal span,\n  - how close its switching rate is to an ideal switching rate,\n  - a reward for varied three-flip chunks,\n  - a penalty for sequences built by an obvious copying or mirror rule.\n\n  What differs between people is how engaged they are, not how they see randomness. Each person has their own share of trials where they don't look at the sequences and just click their usual side.\n- **`candidate.py`**: The randomness impression uses one set of parameters for everyone. Each person gets their own lapse rate, set up as a non-centred logit-normal over 400 participant slots. On a lapse trial they pick by their own side habit. So `p_left = (1−lapse)·sigmoid(evidence) + lapse·p_side`.\n\n**Why this hypothesis:** The critique's one finding that survives the FDR correction (q = 0.048) is that people differ in how often they agree with the majority more than the best model predicts. In the current models, all the differences between people are in perception: their own ideal span and their own sensitivities. This model tests the opposite idea. Everyone ranks the pairs the same way, and people differ only in how far their choices drift toward their side habit. Disengaged people would agree with the majority less on every pair, by the same amount in probability, rather than just responding more weakly.\n\n**Check result:** The short test fit (100 draws, 1 chain) gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −3819.2. Two warnings came with it:\n- **PSIS-LOO unreliable:** 5.5% of trials (420 of 7,680) had a high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance. If the full fit shows the same, the model's ELPD will be flagged as untrustworthy, as `shared_indifference_band_tally_switch`'s already is.\n- **Convergence not checked:** a single-chain fit can't report R-hat. Convergence, novelty against the other models, and the 30-minute fit limit will only be tested at admission.\n\nThat −3819 is about 400 nats behind the current best model (−3413.6). It comes from a very short fit and may change, but taken at face value this model would lose clearly."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3453,"cache_read_input_tokens":77358,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3453,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86b9434a-d3fe-45e5-afb4-a9aa4492bfde","uuid":"b8e18395-ad7f-423c-8cf5-74ae3647df82","timestamp":"2026-10-04T00:10:29.930Z","request_id":"req_011CfgE6cbKQbJikvnAAAGJV"}
